---
/**
 * The seller's money, under the split model: where it comes from, when, and the account it lands in.
 *
 * ── What this screen stopped being, 2026-08-21 ──
 * It used to be the seller's half of a payout WE made: what was payable now, what was still on hold
 * and why, a table splitting the waiting money by reason, and four rules explaining a fifteen-day
 * clock. Every one of those existed because the buyer's money passed through the platform and we
 * decided when to send it on. Under the split model it never arrives here — the processor captures
 * each store's share into that seller's own account at the moment of the charge, deducts our
 * distribution fee inside the same transaction, and credits the seller on its own schedule
 * (GO_LIVE §3.1.0). There is no hold to explain, no release date to promise and no transfer to
 * forecast, so the screen that explained them is three things instead of nine.
 *
 * **The bank and business form stays, and its job changed rather than ended.** It is no longer
 * where we learn how to pay a seller; it is what we hand the processor to open his merchant
 * account, so the same six fields now gate his ability to SELL rather than his ability to be paid.
 * That is a stronger gate, and it is why the owner chose (2026-08-21) to ask for them on the
 * store-opening card, skippable — `payout-details.ts`'s header carries the rule, and both surfaces
 * still write through `parsePayoutDetails` so neither can half-save an account.
 *
 * **The gate is built (2026-08-23).** `/api/checkout` refuses a store whose seller has no APPROVED
 * merchant account, in the pre-pass, before any stock moves (`seller-merchant.ts#merchantBlockFor`).
 * So this panel now carries the one thing a seller must not have to discover from a shopper: that
 * his shop cannot take an order, and what is standing in the way. Rendered only when a clearing
 * provider is configured at all — with none, nothing is blocked and a status line about it would be
 * a sentence about a rule that is not in force.
 *
 * **One account, one registered business** — one ח״פ, one bank account, one clearing account and
 * ONE standing order, however many shops. What is NOT one of them, since 2026-08-24, is the plan:
 * each shop carries its own fee and its own commission rate, and the standing order's amount is the
 * sum of the shops on the site (`lib/store-plan.ts`). A seller with a second ח״פ opens a second
 * account; `DB_MIGRATION_PLAN.md` §10 records why, and it is closed rather than pending.
 */
import type { PayoutDetails } from '../../lib/payout-details.js';
import GoLiveSteps from './GoLiveSteps.astro';
import OwnClearingCard from './OwnClearingCard.astro';
import SubscriptionCard from './SubscriptionCard.astro';
import type { PublishHold } from '../../lib/store-publication.js';
import type { SellerTierId } from '../../lib/pricing.js';
import type { StoreFeeLine } from '../../lib/store-plan.js';
import type { MerchantKyc } from '../../lib/merchant-kyc.js';
import type { ClearingStatus } from '../../lib/seller-merchant.js';
import type { ClearingProvider } from '../../lib/clearing-providers.js';
import type { getT } from '../../i18n/index.js';

interface Props {
  seller: PayoutDetails;
  /** More than one store, so the "these details cover all of them" line is worth saying. */
  multiStore: boolean;
  /** Whether this seller's stores can take money, and what is in the way if not.
   *
   *  **`null` means no clearing provider is configured**, which is dev and the pre-gateway window
   *  GO_LIVE §7 plans — nothing is blocked, so a line about being blocked would describe a rule
   *  that is not in force. The block renders only when there is really something to say. */
  clearing: ClearingStatus | null;
  /** ── The seller's OWN clearing account (SaaS shape, `docs/pivot-saas.md`) ──
   *  Ungated, unlike `clearing` above: that one describes the merchant account WE open for him
   *  through PayMe and is null whenever PayMe is not configured, which is every environment in this
   *  shape. This is the account he already has, and it is the first thing on the money screen
   *  because it is what decides whether his shop can be paid at all. */
  ownClearing: {
    providers: ClearingProvider[];
    currentId: string | null;
    onFile: Record<string, { set: boolean; hint: string }>;
    missing: string[];
  };
  /** What PayMe require about the business, as far as it has been filled in. Rendered under the
   *  bank block (owner, 2026-08-23) — one screen owns everything to do with money, and the
   *  "your shop is not live" card on the overview links straight here. */
  kyc: Partial<MerchantKyc>;
  /** The merchant category derived from the shop's own categories, or null when their list has no
   *  row for this trade. Null is what makes the form render the field at all. */
  derivedCategory: string | null;
  /** True once nothing is outstanding — the fields collapse into one line with an edit button. */
  kycComplete: boolean;
  /** What is still holding the shop off the site. Empty means it is live, and the go-live screen
   *  does not render at all. */
  holds: PublishHold[];
  /** The shop the dashboard is currently on — the one the go-live screen is about. Since 2026-08-24
   *  the plan and the monthly line belong to the SHOP (`lib/store-plan.ts`), so this screen cannot
   *  be about "the account" any more. Null only for a seller who has not created one yet, in which
   *  case there is nothing to put live and the screen does not render. */
  store: { id: string; name: string; tier?: string; publishedAt?: string } | null;
  /** Every plan's display name, for the picker inside the last step. Lives in the pricing copy
   *  rather than the dashboard's, so it is handed down rather than looked up — no component below
   *  this one reaches into another screen's translations. */
  /** The plan's NAME for the current shop, same rule. */
  planName: string;
  /** What is on file, and whether PayMe are actually billing it. Both come from the server so this
   *  card can never disagree with the publication gate about whether the seller is paying.
   *  `storeFees` is what the standing order was last set from — the breakdown behind the total. */
  subscription: { status: number | null; nextCharge?: string; endsAt?: string; priceAgorot?: number; storeFees?: StoreFeeLine[]; cardSavedAt?: string; providerRef?: string } | null;
  subscriptionPaying: boolean;
  /** Whose card the monthly fee is taken from — the seller's own account details, handed down so
   *  the card fields never ask him for anything he has already given us. */
  payer: { name: string; email: string; phone: string };
  d: ReturnType<typeof getT>['dashboard'];
}
const { seller, multiStore, clearing, ownClearing, kyc, derivedCategory, kycComplete, holds, store, planName, subscription, subscriptionPaying, payer, d } = Astro.props;

/** A card is on file and PayMe have nothing yet — the state a seller spends the clearing review in
 *  (`lib/subscription-arm.ts`). Derived from the same two facts the server derives it from, rather
 *  than passed as a third flag that could disagree with them. */
const cardOnFile = !!subscription?.cardSavedAt && !subscription.providerRef;

/** Whether THIS shop is one of the lines the standing order pays for. A paying seller who has just
 *  opened a second shop clears every account-level hold and still owes for this one — the case that
 *  would otherwise put a shop on the site for free (`lib/store-plan.ts`). */
const storeInPlan = !!store && (subscription?.storeFees ?? []).some((f) => f.storeId === store.id);

/** Whether the go-live screen has anything left to say about THIS shop — **the same single
 *  condition it computes for itself**, so the two can never both render or both vanish. It is the
 *  shop being on the site and nothing else: keyed off the STEPS instead, a seller who had finished
 *  them all saw neither screen while his shop was still going up. A cancelled subscription inside
 *  its paid period lands here too, which is right — the shop is up, and what he needs is the manage
 *  card (where the renew button is), not a three-step wizard. */
const goLiveDone = !!store?.publishedAt;

/* `TYPE_LABEL`, `bankLine` and `businessSummary` went with the bank form they composed. */
---

<div id="payouts-root">
  <div class="dash-panel-head mb-4 py-[0.6rem] border-b [border-color:var(--color-border)]">
    <h2 class="m-0 text-base font-semibold">{d.payTitle}</h2>
    <p class="muted text-[0.82rem] m-0 mt-1">{d.paySubtitle}</p>
  </div>

  {/* ── His own clearing account, FIRST on the money screen ──
      Every other block here answers "what happens to money that has already moved". This one
      answers whether it can move at all, and a seller who has not connected an account has no
      reason to read anything below it yet. `OwnClearingCard.astro`'s header carries the rest. */}
  <OwnClearingCard
    providers={ownClearing.providers}
    currentId={ownClearing.currentId}
    onFile={ownClearing.onFile}
    missing={ownClearing.missing}
    d={d}
  />

  {/* ── The transfers strip is GONE (2026-09-08) ──
      It answered *"how much is the processor about to move into my bank"*, read live from PayMe.
      Under the SaaS shape the buyer's money is already IN his account at the moment of the charge —
      it never passes through anyone, so there is no transfer to be pending and no date to give.
      `lib/seller-transfers.ts` and `scripts/dashboard/transfers.ts` go with it. */}

  {/* ── The per-sale fee card LEFT this tab on 2026-08-26 (owner, סשן א׳ §1) ──
      *"העמלות על המכירות לא צריכות להופיע שם, אלא בלשונית דוח״ות, כדו״ח עמלות. כולל כל העמלות
      שהיוזר משלם."* It listed the last six charges with their fee split, which is the right
      information twice in the wrong place: this tab answers *"how much money is coming to me"*, and
      six rows is a sample rather than a record. A fee is an EXPENSE, and an expense belongs where a
      period can be chosen and a CSV exported — `ReportsPanel.astro`, report `fees`.

      Its hint went with it, and was the owner's §4 in the same breath (*"מיותר ולא מובן"*): it read
      *"המספרים של חברת הסליקה, אחרי מע״מ על העמלות. חיובים חודשיים על החשבון עצמו מופיעים אצלה"* —
      two disclaimers about a table, above the table, in the vocabulary of the processor's back
      office rather than the seller's. What was true in it (which fees this covers and which it
      cannot) is said by the fee report's own summary, where it describes a document. */}

  {/* ── PayMe's invoicing add-on is GONE (2026-09-08) ──
      It let a seller switch on PayMe issuing the buyer's invoice in his name, billed to him. It was
      an add-on ON A MERCHANT ACCOUNT WE OPENED FOR HIM, and we do not open one any more — his
      invoicing is a matter between him and his own provider. It had never rendered on any of our
      merchants anyway: PayMe never provisioned the service. */}

  {/**
    * ── The clearing status USED to be announced here, and it is not any more (2026-08-25) ──
    *
    * The page read: "חשבון סליקה … / פרטי בנק … / פרטי העסק לחברת הסליקה" — the same subject twice
    * with the bank block wedged between them (owner: *"לא הבנתי את הסדר הלא מסודר בדף הזה ואת ריבוי
    * הסקשנים"*). It was a status line about a process that now has a whole screen of its own, so
    * it said the same thing worse and a paragraph earlier.
    *
    * `GoLiveSteps` below is the one place that answers it, in the order a seller walks it. The
    * signup link the block carried moved with it, into the approval step that is about that wait.
    */}

  {/* Directly under the bank block, because it is the same errand: the bank account says where the
      money goes and this says who the business is, and PayMe need both before they will open an
      account at all. Rendered only when a clearing provider is configured — with none, nothing is
      blocked and a form about being blocked would be a form about a rule that is not in force. */}
  {/* ONE screen for the whole errand, directly under the bank block — the details form and the
      subscription action live inside it now, as the steps that own them, rather than as two more
      cards beside it (owner, 2026-08-24). It renders nothing once the shop is live. */}
  {/* ── Once the shop is UP, the subscription is a thing to manage rather than a step ──
      The go-live screen renders only while something is still holding the shop off the site, and
      the subscription card lived inside it — so from the moment a seller succeeded, the card
      vanished and with it the next-charge date, the breakdown and **the cancel button**. He was
      paying us monthly with no screen that admitted it. Found in the review of 2026-08-24, in code
      that had been written the same day: the two states are complementary and exactly one of them
      renders, which is also why this is not a second copy of the same card on any screen. */}
  {clearing && store && goLiveDone && (
    <SubscriptionCard
      storeId={store.id}
      tier={store.tier as SellerTierId | undefined}
      planName={planName}
      subscription={subscription}
      paying={subscriptionPaying}
      storeInPlan={storeInPlan}
      cardOnFile={cardOnFile}
      payer={payer}
      businessType={seller.businessType}
      d={d}
    />
  )}

  {clearing && store && (
    <GoLiveSteps
      holds={holds}
      store={store}
      storeInPlan={storeInPlan}
      cardOnFile={cardOnFile}
      payer={payer}
      kyc={kyc}
      derivedCategory={derivedCategory}
      kycComplete={kycComplete}
      businessId={seller.businessId}
      businessType={seller.businessType}
      clearing={clearing}
      planName={planName}
      subscription={subscription}
      subscriptionPaying={subscriptionPaying}
      {...(clearing.signupLink ? { signupLink: clearing.signupLink } : {})}
      d={d}
    />
  )}


  {/* AFTER the go-live screen, not before it. This is where the seller's money LANDS — a fact
      about being paid, not a step in getting the shop up — and putting it first made the page open
      on a form whose purpose the reader had not been told yet. `mt-6` because two bordered cards
      flush against each other read as one thing with a line through it (owner, 2026-08-25:
      *"צריך ריווחים בין הסקשנים"*). */}
  {/* ── The bank-account form is GONE (owner, 2026-09-08: *"למה עדיין צריך פרטי חשבון בנק?"*) ──
      Because there is nothing left for it to answer. It was never our payout instruction: since
      2026-08-21 it was what we handed the processor to open a merchant account FOR the seller
      (`payment-payme.ts#create-seller` takes `seller_bank_code`/`_branch`/`_account`). We open no
      account and we move no money to him — every shekel a buyer pays lands in his OWN clearing
      account at his OWN provider, whose details he pasted at the top of this screen. Asking a
      seller for his bank account to enable something that never happens is the plainest possible
      way to lose his trust on the money screen.

      The COLUMNS stay (`sellers.bank_*`, `lib/payout-details.ts`) and so does the API route: the
      split model is switched off, not deleted, and the day it is switched on this form comes back
      rather than being rebuilt. What was removed is the ASKING. */}

  {/* ── "התמחור שלכם" is gone (owner, 2026-09-08: *"נשמע לי מיותר"*) ──
      It was a closed accordion listing what a seller is charged, and it earned its place when that
      was five things from two parties: a plan fee, a per-sale commission, the processor's fixed ₪1
      and monthly minimum, a setup fee, and invoicing. Every one of those went with the split model
      — he pays one monthly price and his own processor bills him separately for clearing, under an
      agreement we are not party to. An accordion whose contents are one number that is already on
      the card above it is furniture. */}

  {/* ── "איך זה עובד" was removed on 2026-08-26 (owner, סשן א׳ §3) ──
      *"החלק של איך זה עובד לא צריך להיות שם בכלל. אפשר לרשום את היועבר בעשירי לכל חודש תחת היתרה
      לתשלום הקרוב, בקצרה."* Four question-and-answer pairs at the foot of a money screen is a help
      article that has wandered onto a dashboard: the one fact a seller actually came back for — WHEN
      the money moves — is now the line under the balance itself (`payTransferSchedule`), where it
      is read at the moment the question occurs. What each fee IS, and how much of it there is, is
      the pricing section below and the fee report on the Reports tab; those are the two screens
      that can answer it with numbers rather than with prose. */}
</div>
